---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Pyramid vs TriPeaks: Which Quick Solitaire Wins? | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Pyramid and TriPeaks both clear a pretty layout fast — but one pairs cards to thirteen and the other rides a rank-by-rank streak. How they differ and which to play.';
const updated = '2026-06-24';
const heading = 'Pyramid vs TriPeaks';
const subtitle =
  'Two fast, good-looking solitaires that play in minutes — but in completely different ways. Here is how to choose.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Pyramid vs TriPeaks', href: '/guides/pyramid-vs-tripeaks/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the main difference between Pyramid and TriPeaks?',
    a: 'Pyramid is a pairing game: you remove pairs of exposed cards that add up to 13. TriPeaks is a sequence game: you clear cards one at a time by picking any exposed card exactly one rank above or below the card in your waste pile, building long streaks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is easier to win?',
    a: 'TriPeaks tends to feel more winnable because a single good streak can clear large chunks of the board, and rank wraps from King to Ace and back. Pyramid is often tighter — the matching partner you need can get stranded — though both are stock-limited and include deals that cannot be cleared.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is faster?',
    a: 'Both are quick — a round of either usually takes only a few minutes, which is what makes them great for a short break. TriPeaks often plays a touch faster once a streak gets rolling.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Pyramid vs TriPeaks: Which Quick Solitaire Wins?',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', '@id': 'https://cardhearth.com/#jared', name: 'Jared Waterworth', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/pyramid-vs-tripeaks/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/tripeaks/', label: 'Play TriPeaks' }}
>
  <div class="tldr">
    <p class="tldr-title">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Play <a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a></strong> if you like puzzling out pairs that add up to 13 and planning which card to free next.</li>
      <li><strong>Play <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a></strong> if you like fast, satisfying streaks that chain across the board.</li>
      <li><strong>Both</strong> are quick, stock-limited, and a little luck-dependent — perfect for a short break.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    <a href="/pyramid/">Pyramid</a> and <a href="/tripeaks/">TriPeaks</a> are the comfort-food
    solitaires: pretty layouts, short games, easy to pick up. But under the hood they're built on two
    completely different ideas — one is about <em>pairing</em>, the other about <em>sequencing</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>How each one works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pyramid</strong> deals 28 cards in a pyramid. You remove exposed cards in pairs that sum to
    <strong>13</strong> (a 5 with an 8, a 4 with a 9; Kings are worth 13 and go alone). The catch is
    that a card is only available once both cards covering it are gone, so the order you clear things in
    decides whether the partner you need ever comes free. <strong>TriPeaks</strong> deals three
    overlapping peaks. You clear one exposed card at a time by taking any card exactly <strong>one rank
    above or below</strong> the top card of your waste pile — and rank <em>wraps</em>, so a King can
    follow an Ace. String those together and one move flows into the next.
  </p>

  <h2>The feel of each game</h2>
  <p>
    Pyramid is the more <strong>deliberate</strong> of the two — a small optimisation puzzle where you're
    constantly asking "if I take this, do I strand its partner?" TriPeaks is the more <strong>kinetic</strong>:
    when a streak gets going it's genuinely exciting to watch a single run peel cards off all three peaks.
    Pyramid rewards foresight; TriPeaks rewards spotting the longest chain.
  </p>

  <h2>Difficulty and winnability</h2>
  <p>
    Both are stock-limited, so both include deals that simply can't be cleared — that's the nature of
    these games, not a flaw. TriPeaks often feels more forgiving because one good streak clears so much
    at once, while Pyramid can lock up when a needed partner is buried. Skilful order of play wins many
    marginal hands in each. The <a href="/guides/pyramid-solitaire-strategy/">Pyramid strategy</a> and
    <a href="/guides/tripeaks-solitaire-strategy/">TriPeaks strategy</a> guides show how, and
    <a href="/guides/are-solitaire-games-winnable/">are solitaire games winnable?</a> covers where they
    sit overall.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <p>
    Pick <strong>Pyramid</strong> for a thoughtful little pairing puzzle, and <strong>TriPeaks</strong>
    when you want quick momentum and satisfying streaks. They're different enough that most people enjoy
    keeping both around for a spare few minutes — and if you'd rather a game where every deal is
    guaranteed beatable, try <a href="/sudoku/">Sudoku</a> or <a href="/mahjong/">Mahjong</a> instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }
</Article>
